e i think  there's there's always something to be  said about getting together in person  breaking bread going out exploring a  city exploring a country together as a  group and it would be  absolutely awful to think that we  weren't going to do that again so  i would definitely not want to go down  that road and just say we're going to do  it all online from now on but at the  same time  there's an inclusiveness about doing it  globally and  also taking this 10-day period  is really quite exciting because we're  not restricted by times we're not  restricted by space and by renting  certain areas to say okay you have to  speak this amount of time  at this time on this day and that's it  if it doesn't happen then if that's it's  gone  whereas right now with this we can say  if your presentation presentation's 10  minutes long it's 10 minutes long  doesn't matter it's not like you have to  sort of occupy the stage and change  things over mics and things it's all  done until ready so we've got a lot of  advantages from this that i think would  be foolish to throw out and not retain  one last question  you mentioned the word inclusiveness and  i've always felt that at our polyglot  gatherings conferences long fest  everyone i meet there  is able to put five six eight nine ten  badges on their chest  indicating the languages they speak  and i've always felt that we are  kind of lim we always limit ourselves to  those people who have already taken the  plunge into  polyglotism and enjoy learning lots of  languages and that there's a whole bunch  of people on the sidelines  that we should be  you know getting into the swimming pool  with us  what  are you doing to get people who maybe  don't even speak one other language or  you know speak one poorly and like what  are we doing to broaden the base beyond  you know hardcore polyglots okay so um i  mean the name the name is the name of  the conference it's the name of the  event right polyglot conference  but we do have a tagline and it says for  everyone who loves language so we don't  even say languages we say f for everyone  who loves language and it's true i mean  you can join the conference even if you  just speak english or you just speak  french or you just speak another  language okay you might not understand  all of the all of the content but  there's always something in there for  you because we have talks from  many many areas so  every year we have themes at the  conference so we've attracted people in  the past say in reykjavik in iceland one  of the themes was  autism and multilingualism so how people  with autism are affected when they have  more than one language in the home  environment or outside  and people came in and showed their  research or their research plans for  phds to discuss that from academia  and we also had people  like daniel tammet come over to talk  about his experience learning icelandic  as somebody with asperger's syndrome now  we attracted people who came to the  conference  who were interested in the autism side  of things  and maybe have been interested a little  bit in languages  and they saw that actually it's really  uh  applicable to them and the people that  were at the event were not these elitist  people who were you know saying oh you  don't speak enough languages or anything  like that and every year because we  change this theme and because we add on  in in slovenia we had a language  learning day  just for people who were learning the  first second or third whatever language  and we invited anyone and everyone to  come so we had a full day for those  people to come and to join in  and just to sort of show them how  approachable we are and you know as well  as i do we're a pretty friendly bunch  and i think oh the friendliness and the  the people are just accepted regardless  of whatever there is not a more  supportive group than the polyglot group  but i guess i guess what i feel you know  for people who love language there's a  lot 